# Our Life is entangled with Information, Truth is scarce, the Internet is Inhumane

*Two instruments, one thesis. One maps what is being said. The other keeps score on who was
right.*

Most tools for handling information optimize for retrieval: you know what you want, they
fetch it. That assumes the hard part is access. It isn't, and hasn't been for two decades.
The hard part is orientation — knowing what the shape of a discourse is before you have read
any of it, and knowing which voices inside it have historically been worth listening to.

Those are two different measurement problems, so this is two systems.

**Infolake** is the map. It crawls a wide set of independent sources, embeds everything,
clusters it, and renders the corpus as a navigable hypergraph. You look at the terrain before
you pick a trail. The bet is that the geometry of a corpus carries information that no
individual document does — where the dense regions are, what sits between them, and what is
conspicuously empty.

**Infoledger** is the scorecard. It ingests forward-looking claims that public figures
already made voluntarily and in public, compiles them into falsifiable propositions, resolves
them against outcomes when the horizon arrives, and aggregates per-source track records.
Nobody opts in. Prediction markets require participation and fact-checking arrives after the
fact; this sits in the gap between them.

The shared substrate is **infocrawl**, a contract-based multi-source acquisition layer, and a
common hypergraph store. The shared discipline is that both systems are built to be honest
about their own uncertainty — which, it turns out, is most of the engineering.

---

**Why this stopped being a side project.** It began as a personal instrument: a better way
for one person to select and absorb information. It kept growing load-bearing parts — a
crawler fleet, an adapter contract, a taxonomy backbone, an inference layer with real
statistical commitments — until the honest description was no longer "a project" but a small
research program with two product-shaped ends. It now runs on its own domain and its own
infrastructure.

Status: active. Infolake has a working pipeline and UI. Infoledger is at the
specification-and-validation stage, deliberately: the data-integrity invariants have to be
right *before* collection starts, because a ledger collected without them cannot be repaired
afterwards.

